# Architectural Survey & Design Specification: `crates/flightreader-server`

**Document Version:** 1.0.0  
**Target Milestone:** OpenFlightReader Phase 3 Milestone M2  
**Author:** Explorer M2-1 (Axum Server Architecture & Endpoints)  
**Status:** Complete Architectural Baseline  

---

## 1. Executive Summary & Architectural Overview

`crates/flightreader-server` delivers the lightweight, 100% offline local web server and visualization backend for OpenFlightReader. Built on top of `flightreader-core` (binary log parsing, crypto, and geodetics) and `flightreader-export` (synchronized multi-sensor time-series aggregation and geospatial models), `flightreader-server` replaces the proprietary Windows-only, cloud-tethered runtime of commercial FlightReader with a cross-platform, air-gapped web daemon.

```
                           Raw Binary Flight Log (.txt / .DAT)
                                         │
                                         ▼
                            crates/flightreader-core
                      (Header, CRC64, XOR, AES-256, Demux)
                                         │
                                         ▼
                            crates/flightreader-export
                      (TelemetryAggregator -> FlightDataset)
                                         │
                                         ▼
                            crates/flightreader-server
                     ┌───────────────────┴───────────────────┐
                     ▼                                       ▼
             REST / WebSocket APIs                  Embedded Offline Assets
      - GET  /api/v1/health                  - rust-embed 8.5 bundling
      - GET  /api/v1/flight/info             - Strict Air-Gap CSP Header
      - GET  /api/v1/flight/points           - Offline Leaflet & Canvas Fallback
      - POST /api/v1/upload                  - ADI Horizon & Dual-Stick Visualizer
      - GET  /api/v1/ws (60 FPS Scrubber)    - 12-Cell Battery Matrix Widget
```

### Key Architectural Invariants
1. **Zero External Cloud Calls (100% Air-Gapped)**: Under no operational condition does the server or frontend initiate an outbound network request to any vendor API, CDN, analytics server, or tile provider. A strict Content-Security-Policy (CSP) enforces this at the browser level.
2. **Columnar In-Memory Serialization (ADR-004)**: Telemetry data points are serialized into parallel typed vectors rather than an array of individual objects. This achieves $O(1)$ timeline access, reduces browser memory footprint from $>100\text{ MB}$ to $<12\text{ MB}$, and enables rock-solid 60 FPS timeline scrubbing.
3. **Single Binary Portability**: Frontend dashboard assets (HTML, CSS, JS, icons) are embedded directly into the Rust binary at compile time via `rust-embed 8.5`.
4. **Safety & Robustness**: The server crate strictly enforces `#![deny(unsafe_code)]` with zero `unsafe` blocks, and handles corrupted or truncated uploads via typed error responses rather than process aborts.

---

## 2. Dependency Specification (`Cargo.toml`)

The server crate resides in `crates/flightreader-server` and participates in the root workspace.

```toml
[package]
name = "flightreader-server"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"
authors = ["OpenFlightReader Contributors"]
license = "MIT OR Apache-2.0"
description = "Lightweight, 100% offline Axum web server and interactive telemetry dashboard for OpenFlightReader."

[dependencies]
# Workspace crates
flightreader-core = { path = "../flightreader-core" }
flightreader-export = { path = "../flightreader-export" }

# HTTP & Web Framework (Axum 0.7)
axum = { version = "0.7", features = ["ws", "multipart", "json"] }
tokio = { version = "1.37", features = ["full"] }
tower = { version = "0.5", features = ["util"] }
tower-http = { version = "0.5", features = ["cors", "trace", "fs", "set-header", "limit"] }

# Compile-Time Asset Embedding
rust-embed = { version = "8.5", features = ["mime-guess", "compression"] }
mime_guess = "2.0"

# Serialization & Error Handling
serde = { version = "1.0", features = ["derive"] }
serde_json = "1.0"
thiserror = "2.0"

# Async & Networking Utilities
futures-util = { version = "0.3", default-features = false, features = ["sink", "std"] }
tracing = "0.1"
tracing-subscriber = { version = "0.3", features = ["env-filter"] }

[dev-dependencies]
http-body-util = "0.1"
tokio-test = "0.4"
tempfile = "3.17"
```

### Dependency Rationale
- **`axum 0.7`**: High ergonomics, native Tokio ecosystem integration, hyper 1.0 support, zero-cost routing, and built-in WebSocket (`ws`) and `multipart` extractors.
- **`tower-http 0.5`**: Standardized production middleware:
  - `cors`: Permissive local origin support for standalone web and desktop development.
  - `trace`: Structured HTTP request/response logging via `tracing`.
  - `set-header`: Injection of security and CSP headers.
  - `limit`: `DefaultBodyLimit` configuration allowing uploads of large drone blackbox `.DAT` logs ($>100\text{ MB}$).
- **`rust-embed 8.5`**: Compiles assets from `crates/flightreader-server/assets/` into binary read-only memory with gzip compression support and MIME resolution.
- **`futures-util 0.3`**: Provides `StreamExt` and `SinkExt` for splitting Axum WebSockets into concurrent read/write streams.

---

## 3. Server Core & Configuration (`src/lib.rs` & `src/error.rs`)

### 3.1 Strict Safety Directive
`src/lib.rs` begins with:
```rust
#![deny(unsafe_code)]
#![warn(missing_docs)]
```
No `unsafe` blocks are permitted in any server route, state management, or asset handler.

### 3.2 Server Configuration (`ServerConfig`)
The configuration struct defines runtime parameters supplied by the CLI `serve` subcommand:

```rust
use std::path::PathBuf;
use flightreader_export::model::FlightDataset;

/// Configuration parameters for launching the offline Axum server.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct ServerConfig {
    /// IP address or interface to bind (e.g., "127.0.0.1" for localhost, "0.0.0.0" for LAN).
    pub host: String,
    /// TCP port to bind (default: 8080).
    pub port: u16,
    /// Optional path to an MBTiles vector/raster basemap SQLite archive.
    pub mbtiles_path: Option<PathBuf>,
    /// Optional directory containing extracted offline map tiles (`{z}/{x}/{y}.png`).
    pub tile_dir: Option<PathBuf>,
    /// Pre-loaded flight dataset (e.g. provided via CLI argument `flightreader serve <FILE>`).
    pub initial_dataset: Option<FlightDataset>,
}

impl Default for ServerConfig {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            host: "127.0.0.1".to_string(),
            port: 8080,
            mbtiles_path: None,
            tile_dir: None,
            initial_dataset: None,
        }
    }
}
```

### 3.3 Server Error Hierarchy (`src/error.rs`)
Centralized error handling with automatic conversion into Axum HTTP responses:

```rust
use axum::{
    http::StatusCode,
    response::{IntoResponse, Response},
    Json,
};
use serde_json::json;
use thiserror::Error;

/// Central error type for flightreader-server operations.
#[derive(Debug, Error)]
pub enum ServerError {
    #[error("I/O error: {0}")]
    Io(#[from] std::io::Error),

    #[error("Core log parsing error: {0}")]
    Core(#[from] flightreader_core::error::FlightReaderError),

    #[error("Export aggregation error: {0}")]
    Export(#[from] flightreader_export::error::ExportError),

    #[error("JSON serialization error: {0}")]
    Json(#[from] serde_json::Error),

    #[error("Flight dataset not found: {0}")]
    NotFound(String),

    #[error("Bad request: {0}")]
    BadRequest(String),

    #[error("Invalid multipart payload: {0}")]
    Multipart(String),

    #[error("Internal server error: {0}")]
    Internal(String),
}

impl IntoResponse for ServerError {
    fn into_response(self) -> Response {
        let (status, error_code, message) = match &self {
            ServerError::NotFound(msg) => (StatusCode::NOT_FOUND, "NOT_FOUND", msg.clone()),
            ServerError::BadRequest(msg) | ServerError::Multipart(msg) => {
                (StatusCode::BAD_REQUEST, "BAD_REQUEST", msg.clone())
            }
            ServerError::Core(err) => (
                StatusCode::UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY,
                "CORRUPT_LOG_PAYLOAD",
                err.to_string(),
            ),
            ServerError::Export(err) => (
                StatusCode::UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY,
                "AGGREGATION_FAILED",
                err.to_string(),
            ),
            ServerError::Io(err) => (
                StatusCode::INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR,
                "IO_ERROR",
                err.to_string(),
            ),
            ServerError::Json(err) => (
                StatusCode::INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR,
                "SERIALIZATION_ERROR",
                err.to_string(),
            ),
            ServerError::Internal(msg) => (
                StatusCode::INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR,
                "INTERNAL_ERROR",
                msg.clone(),
            ),
        };

        let body = Json(json!({
            "error": message,
            "code": error_code,
            "status": status.as_u16(),
        }));

        (status, body).into_response()
    }
}
```

### 3.4 Router Construction (`build_app`)
The router links all REST routes, WebSocket handlers, CORS middleware, body limits, and embedded asset fallback:

```rust
use axum::{
    extract::DefaultBodyLimit,
    routing::{get, post},
    Router,
};
use tower_http::cors::{Any, CorsLayer};
use tower_http::trace::TraceLayer;

/// Builds the complete Axum router configured with middleware and shared state.
pub fn build_app(state: AppState) -> Router {
    // Permissive CORS for local cross-origin development and desktop integration
    let cors = CorsLayer::new()
        .allow_origin(Any)
        .allow_methods(Any)
        .allow_headers(Any);

    // 256 MB upload limit to support high-frequency blackbox .DAT files
    let body_limit = DefaultBodyLimit::max(256 * 1024 * 1024);

    Router::new()
        // API v1 Endpoints
        .route("/api/v1/health", get(routes::health::health_check))
        .route("/api/v1/flight/info", get(routes::flight::get_flight_info))
        .route("/api/v1/flight/points", get(routes::flight::get_flight_points))
        .route("/api/v1/upload", post(routes::upload::upload_flight_log))
        .route("/api/v1/ws", get(routes::ws::ws_handler))
        // Layer middleware
        .layer(body_limit)
        .layer(cors)
        .layer(TraceLayer::new_for_http())
        .with_state(state)
        // Static assets and fallback
        .fallback(assets::static_handler)
}
```

### 3.5 Server Launch & Graceful Shutdown (`start_server`)
```rust
use tokio::net::TcpListener;

/// Starts the HTTP/WebSocket server listening on the configured socket address.
pub async fn start_server(config: ServerConfig) -> Result<(), ServerError> {
    let addr = format!("{}:{}", config.host, config.port);
    let listener = TcpListener::bind(&addr).await?;
    tracing::info!("OpenFlightReader server listening on http://{}", addr);

    let state = AppState::new(config);
    let app = build_app(state);

    axum::serve(listener, app)
        .with_graceful_shutdown(shutdown_signal())
        .await?;

    tracing::info!("Server shut down gracefully");
    Ok(())
}

async fn shutdown_signal() {
    let _ = tokio::signal::ctrl_c().await;
    tracing::info!("Received Ctrl-C signal, initiating graceful shutdown...");
}
```

---

## 4. Application State & Concurrency Model (`src/state.rs`)

### 4.1 State Structure
`AppState` is thread-safe, cheaply cloneable (`Arc`), and encapsulates the active flight dataset alongside the WebSocket broadcast bus:

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use tokio::sync::{broadcast, RwLock};
use flightreader_export::model::FlightDataset;
use crate::lib::ServerConfig;
use crate::routes::ws::WsScrubMessage;

/// Shared application state across all request handlers and WebSocket connections.
#[derive(Clone)]
pub struct AppState {
    /// In-memory active flight dataset, protected by an asynchronous read-write lock.
    pub dataset: Arc<RwLock<Option<FlightDataset>>>,
    /// Immutable server launch configuration.
    pub config: Arc<ServerConfig>,
    /// Broadcast channel for real-time timeline scrubber synchronization (capacity: 128).
    pub ws_broadcaster: broadcast::Sender<WsScrubMessage>,
    /// Timestamp when server was started.
    pub start_time: std::time::Instant,
}

impl AppState {
    /// Creates a new `AppState` instance initialized from the given configuration.
    pub fn new(config: ServerConfig) -> Self {
        let (ws_broadcaster, _) = broadcast::channel(128);
        let dataset = Arc::new(RwLock::new(config.initial_dataset.clone()));

        Self {
            dataset,
            config: Arc::new(config),
            ws_broadcaster,
            start_time: std::time::Instant::now(),
        }
    }

    /// Convenience helper to set the active dataset and broadcast an update event.
    pub async fn set_dataset(&self, new_dataset: FlightDataset) {
        let count = new_dataset.points.len();
        let duration = new_dataset.metadata.duration_seconds;
        let log_type = new_dataset.metadata.log_type.clone();

        {
            let mut lock = self.dataset.write().await;
            *lock = Some(new_dataset);
        }

        let _ = self.ws_broadcaster.send(WsScrubMessage::DatasetLoaded {
            total_points: count,
            duration_seconds: duration,
            log_type,
        });
    }

    /// Checks if a dataset is currently loaded.
    pub async fn has_dataset(&self) -> bool {
        self.dataset.read().await.is_some()
    }
}
```

### 4.2 Concurrency & Deadlock Prevention
- All dataset reads (`read().await`) take read-locks that release immediately once data is cloned or summarized into response buffers.
- Dataset updates (`write().await`) occur only during the upload endpoint lifecycle. Writing takes $<10\ \mu\text{s}$ because `Option<FlightDataset>` pointer swap is atomic in memory.
- No locks are held across I/O awaits or WebSocket sends, guaranteeing zero deadlock conditions.

---

## 5. REST API Specifications & Routing (`src/routes/`)

### 5.1 Health Check: `GET /api/v1/health`
Verifies server liveness, package version, uptime, and dataset presence.

```rust
// src/routes/health.rs
use axum::{extract::State, Json};
use serde::Serialize;
use crate::state::AppState;

#[derive(Debug, Serialize)]
pub struct HealthResponse {
    pub status: &'static str,
    pub service: &'static str,
    pub version: &'static str,
    pub has_flight_data: bool,
    pub point_count: usize,
    pub uptime_seconds: u64,
}

pub async fn health_check(State(state): State<AppState>) -> Json<HealthResponse> {
    let (has_flight_data, point_count) = {
        let lock = state.dataset.read().await;
        match &*lock {
            Some(ds) => (true, ds.points.len()),
            None => (false, 0),
        }
    };

    Json(HealthResponse {
        status: "ok",
        service: "flightreader-server",
        version: env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION"),
        has_flight_data,
        point_count,
        uptime_seconds: state.start_time.elapsed().as_secs(),
    })
}
```

**Example Response (200 OK):**
```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "service": "flightreader-server",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "has_flight_data": true,
  "point_count": 1420,
  "uptime_seconds": 42
}
```

---

### 5.2 Flight Info: `GET /api/v1/flight/info`
Returns flight metadata, summary statistics, milestone events, and the 2D/3D trajectory bounding box for frontend map auto-zoom.

```rust
// src/routes/flight.rs
use axum::{extract::State, Json};
use serde::Serialize;
use flightreader_export::model::{FlightEvent, FlightMetadata};
use crate::error::ServerError;
use crate::state::AppState;

#[derive(Debug, Serialize)]
pub struct FlightBounds {
    pub min_latitude: f64,
    pub max_latitude: f64,
    pub min_longitude: f64,
    pub max_longitude: f64,
    pub min_altitude_msl: f64,
    pub max_altitude_msl: f64,
}

#[derive(Debug, Serialize)]
pub struct FlightInfoResponse {
    pub metadata: FlightMetadata,
    pub events: Vec<FlightEvent>,
    pub bounds: Option<FlightBounds>,
}

pub async fn get_flight_info(State(state): State<AppState>) -> Result<Json<FlightInfoResponse>, ServerError> {
    let lock = state.dataset.read().await;
    let dataset = lock.as_ref().ok_or_else(|| {
        ServerError::NotFound("No flight dataset is currently loaded on the server".to_string())
    })?;

    // Compute geographic bounding box across points with valid GPS coordinates
    let mut min_lat = 90.0f64;
    let mut max_lat = -90.0f64;
    let mut min_lon = 180.0f64;
    let mut max_lon = -180.0f64;
    let mut min_alt = f64::INFINITY;
    let mut max_alt = f64::NEG_INFINITY;
    let mut valid_coords = 0usize;

    for pt in &dataset.points {
        if pt.latitude != 0.0 || pt.longitude != 0.0 {
            valid_coords += 1;
            min_lat = min_lat.min(pt.latitude);
            max_lat = max_lat.max(pt.latitude);
            min_lon = min_lon.min(pt.longitude);
            max_lon = max_lon.max(pt.longitude);
            min_alt = min_alt.min(pt.altitude_msl);
            max_alt = max_alt.max(pt.altitude_msl);
        }
    }

    let bounds = if valid_coords > 0 {
        Some(FlightBounds {
            min_latitude: min_lat,
            max_latitude: max_lat,
            min_longitude: min_lon,
            max_longitude: max_lon,
            min_altitude_msl: min_alt,
            max_altitude_msl: max_alt,
        })
    } else {
        None
    };

    Ok(Json(FlightInfoResponse {
        metadata: dataset.metadata.clone(),
        events: dataset.events.clone(),
        bounds,
    }))
}
```

---

### 5.3 Columnar Flight Points: `GET /api/v1/flight/points`
**Architectural Rule (ADR-004)**: Delivers telemetry in contiguous columnar JSON arrays rather than individual point objects. This eliminates browser object overhead and allows instant zero-copy wrapping into typed arrays (`Float64Array`, `Float32Array`, `Uint8Array`).

```rust
use axum::extract::Query;
use serde::Deserialize;

#[derive(Debug, Deserialize)]
pub struct PointsQuery {
    /// Optional downsampling rate in Hz (e.g. 1.0, 5.0, 10.0).
    pub rate: Option<f64>,
    /// Optional start elapsed flight time in seconds.
    pub start: Option<f64>,
    /// Optional end elapsed flight time in seconds.
    pub end: Option<f64>,
}

#[derive(Debug, Serialize)]
pub struct ColumnarPointsResponse {
    pub total_points: usize,
    pub indices: Vec<usize>,
    pub timestamps_utc_ms: Vec<i64>,
    pub elapsed_s: Vec<f64>,
    pub fly_time_s: Vec<f64>,

    // Spatial Position & Altitudes
    pub latitude: Vec<f64>,
    pub longitude: Vec<f64>,
    pub altitude_msl: Vec<f64>,
    pub altitude_agl: Vec<f64>,
    pub altitude_baro: Vec<f64>,

    // Kinematics & Velocities
    pub ground_speed_mps: Vec<f64>,
    pub speed_3d_mps: Vec<f64>,
    pub climb_rate_mps: Vec<f64>,
    pub vel_n: Vec<f64>,
    pub vel_e: Vec<f64>,
    pub vel_d: Vec<f64>,
    pub course_deg: Vec<f64>,

    // Attitude (Euler Angles)
    pub pitch_deg: Vec<f64>,
    pub roll_deg: Vec<f64>,
    pub yaw_deg: Vec<f64>,
    pub heading_deg: Vec<f64>,

    // Gimbal
    pub gimbal_pitch_deg: Vec<f64>,
    pub gimbal_roll_deg: Vec<f64>,
    pub gimbal_yaw_deg: Vec<f64>,

    // RC Controller Sticks
    pub rc_aileron: Vec<f64>,
    pub rc_elevator: Vec<f64>,
    pub rc_throttle: Vec<f64>,
    pub rc_rudder: Vec<f64>,

    // Battery & Cell Matrix
    pub battery_pct: Vec<u8>,
    pub battery_voltage_mv: Vec<u32>,
    pub battery_temp_c: Vec<f64>,
    pub cell_voltages_mv: Vec<Vec<u16>>,
    pub cell_deviation_mv: Vec<u16>,

    // Status & Mode
    pub gps_satellites: Vec<u8>,
    pub flight_mode: Vec<String>,
    pub is_motor_on: Vec<bool>,
}

pub async fn get_flight_points(
    State(state): State<AppState>,
    Query(query): Query<PointsQuery>,
) -> Result<Json<ColumnarPointsResponse>, ServerError> {
    let lock = state.dataset.read().await;
    let dataset = lock.as_ref().ok_or_else(|| {
        ServerError::NotFound("No flight dataset is currently loaded on the server".to_string())
    })?;

    let points = &dataset.points;
    let cap = points.len();

    // Pre-allocate columnar vectors to eliminate intermediate re-allocations
    let mut resp = ColumnarPointsResponse {
        total_points: 0,
        indices: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        timestamps_utc_ms: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        elapsed_s: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        fly_time_s: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        latitude: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        longitude: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        altitude_msl: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        altitude_agl: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        altitude_baro: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        ground_speed_mps: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        speed_3d_mps: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        climb_rate_mps: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        vel_n: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        vel_e: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        vel_d: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        course_deg: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        pitch_deg: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        roll_deg: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        yaw_deg: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        heading_deg: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        gimbal_pitch_deg: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        gimbal_roll_deg: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        gimbal_yaw_deg: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        rc_aileron: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        rc_elevator: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        rc_throttle: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        rc_rudder: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        battery_pct: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        battery_voltage_mv: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        battery_temp_c: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        cell_voltages_mv: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        cell_deviation_mv: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        gps_satellites: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        flight_mode: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
        is_motor_on: Vec::with_capacity(cap),
    };

    let start_s = query.start.unwrap_or(0.0);
    let end_s = query.end.unwrap_or(f64::INFINITY);

    for pt in points {
        if pt.elapsed_s < start_s || pt.elapsed_s > end_s {
            continue;
        }

        resp.indices.push(pt.index);
        resp.timestamps_utc_ms.push(pt.timestamp_utc_ms);
        resp.elapsed_s.push(pt.elapsed_s);
        resp.fly_time_s.push(pt.fly_time_s);
        resp.latitude.push(pt.latitude);
        resp.longitude.push(pt.longitude);
        resp.altitude_msl.push(pt.altitude_msl);
        resp.altitude_agl.push(pt.altitude_agl);
        resp.altitude_baro.push(pt.altitude_baro);
        resp.ground_speed_mps.push(pt.ground_speed_mps);
        resp.speed_3d_mps.push(pt.speed_3d_mps);
        resp.climb_rate_mps.push(pt.climb_rate_mps);
        resp.vel_n.push(pt.vel_n);
        resp.vel_e.push(pt.vel_e);
        resp.vel_d.push(pt.vel_d);
        resp.course_deg.push(pt.course_deg);
        resp.pitch_deg.push(pt.pitch_deg);
        resp.roll_deg.push(pt.roll_deg);
        resp.yaw_deg.push(pt.yaw_deg);
        resp.heading_deg.push(pt.heading_deg);
        resp.gimbal_pitch_deg.push(pt.gimbal_pitch_deg);
        resp.gimbal_roll_deg.push(pt.gimbal_roll_deg);
        resp.gimbal_yaw_deg.push(pt.gimbal_yaw_deg);
        resp.rc_aileron.push(pt.rc_aileron);
        resp.rc_elevator.push(pt.rc_elevator);
        resp.rc_throttle.push(pt.rc_throttle);
        resp.rc_rudder.push(pt.rc_rudder);
        resp.battery_pct.push(pt.battery_pct);
        resp.battery_voltage_mv.push(pt.battery_voltage_mv);
        resp.battery_temp_c.push(pt.battery_temp_c);
        resp.cell_voltages_mv.push(pt.cell_voltages_mv.clone());
        resp.cell_deviation_mv.push(pt.cell_deviation_mv);
        resp.gps_satellites.push(pt.gps_satellites);
        resp.flight_mode.push(pt.flight_mode.clone());
        resp.is_motor_on.push(pt.is_motor_on);
    }

    resp.total_points = resp.indices.len();
    Ok(Json(resp))
}
